| 1 | Scan, EXIF, RAW matching, thumbnails | 2–3 days | Every image is catalogued with thumbnails; RAW links are correct on a spot check |
| 2 | Embeddings, text and similar search API | 2 days | Search results look sensible for 20 test queries |
| 3 | Zero-shot tags and duplicates | 1–2 days | Tag counts look plausible; duplicate groups are correct on a spot check |
| 4 | Svelte UI | 3–4 days | All features in §11.2 work against the full archive |
| 5 | Polish and evaluation | 1–2 days | Evaluation note written (§13) |

Total: roughly two to three weeks. The full archive is processed from the start; there is no separate pilot.

## 13. Evaluation

Short and qualitative, recorded in a one-page note:

- 20 text queries: how many of the top 20 results are relevant?
- 10 similar-image searches: are the results useful or just near-copies?
- Tag spot check: look at 30 images per tag for the 10 largest tags; which are reliable, which are noise?
- Timing: full index time and search latency on the development machine.
- Open question: is a Chinese or multilingual CLIP model worth adding for Chinese queries and signage?

## 14. Candidates after the MVP

In rough order of expected value:

1. Manual tag add/remove, stored separately from zero-shot tags.
2. Collections, with export as a list of source paths.
3. A second embedding model for comparison (e.g. a SigLIP-family or multilingual model).
4. Image-prototype tags built from a few example photos, for concepts that text describes badly.
5. Date and camera filters; per-camera clock offset correction.
6. A 2D embedding map (UMAP) for exploration.
7. Captions and OCR through a local vision-language model.

## 15. Decisions before coding

1. Development machine: OS, RAM, and GPU or Apple Silicon.
2. Image formats present, especially whether HEIC needs support.
3. Where RAW files live relative to their JPEGs, to set `raw_search_dirs`.
4. Default CLIP model; `ViT-B-16` is a reasonable start, and a larger model can be tried in milestone 2.
